course: dictionary
lesson: 10845
title: Animals with a backbone
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A vertebrate is an animal with a backbone, also called a spine. Vertebrate can be a noun, as in «vertebrates and invertebrates», or an adjective, as in «vertebrate animals».
explain: «Animals with a backbone are vertebrates: mammals have hair or fur and feed their babies milk; birds have feathers and lay hard-shelled eggs; fish live in water, breathe with gills and most have scales and fins.» These clues tell you what each group looks like.
explain: An animal without a backbone is an invertebrate. The prefix in- means not, so invertebrate means not a vertebrate. When you sort an animal, first ask: does it have a backbone?
fact: A vertebrate is an animal with a backbone.
fact: The five main groups of vertebrates are fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals.
fact: Humans are vertebrates.
fact: An animal without a backbone is an invertebrate.
fact: Scientists sort animals into vertebrates (with a backbone) and invertebrates (without one).
exercise: What does vertebrate mean? | A rock with fossils | An animal without a backbone | *An animal with a backbone | A plant with roots
exercise: Which vertebrate group has hair or fur and feeds its babies milk? | Fish | Amphibians | *Mammals | Birds
exercise: Are humans vertebrates or invertebrates? | Invertebrates | Both, because humans are insects | *Vertebrates | Neither, because humans have no backbone
test: Which of these is NOT one of the five main groups of vertebrates? | Reptiles | *Insects | Fish | Birds
test: «Fish live in water, breathe with gills and most have scales and fins.» What do fish breathe with? | Lungs | Feathers | Fur | *Gills
exam: Which word means an animal without a backbone? | Mammal | *Invertebrate | Fish | Vertebrate
